2026-10-25|Number of Closed Islands

Question:
Given a 2D grid consisting of 0s (land) and 1s (water), return the number of closed islands. An island is considered closed if all its land cells are surrounded by water and it does not touch the grid boundary.

Approach:

1. Traverse every cell in the grid.
2. If a land cell (0) touches the boundary, perform DFS to mark its entire connected island as visited. This island cannot be closed.
3. After processing boundary-connected islands, traverse the remaining grid.
4. Whenever an unvisited land cell is found, increment the closed island count and use DFS to mark all cells belonging to that island.
5. Return the total count.

Solution:

```
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
private:
    int rows, cols;

    void dfs(vector<vector<int>>& grid, int r, int c) {
        if (r < 0 || r >= rows || c < 0 || c >= cols ||
            grid[r][c] == 1) {
            return;
        }

        grid[r][c] = 1;

        dfs(grid, r - 1, c);
        dfs(grid, r + 1, c);
        dfs(grid, r, c - 1);
        dfs(grid, r, c + 1);
    }

public:
    int closedIsland(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {
        rows = grid.size();
        cols = grid[0].size();

        // Remove all islands connected to the boundary.
        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            dfs(grid, r, 0);
            dfs(grid, r, cols - 1);
        }

        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
            dfs(grid, 0, c);
            dfs(grid, rows - 1, c);
        }

        int count = 0;

        // Count the remaining islands.
        for (int r = 1; r < rows - 1; r++) {
            for (int c = 1; c < cols - 1; c++) {
                if (grid[r][c] == 0) {
                    count++;
                    dfs(grid, r, c);
                }
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
};
```

Time Complexity: O(rows × cols), because each cell is visited at most a constant number of times.

Space Complexity: O(rows × cols) in the worst case due to the recursive DFS call stack.
